;; FH-ROUTELINK-007 — the accepted grammar at :on-click is closed.
;;
;; Everywhere else in Freehand, `:on-click [:app/event …]` is ordinary
;; intent data. On this one view it cannot be, and the reason has to be
;; STATED rather than discovered: a route click already produces the ONE
;; routing intent, so a second intent site on the same click would be one
;; user action yielding two semantic events.
;;
;; The failure this closes is silent, which is why it is a law and not a
;; docstring. The routing seam's compatibility signature takes an optional
;; user fn and invokes it, so an event vector arriving there is called as
;; an IFn collection lookup keyed by a MouseEvent, a `v/event` is a
;; deliberately non-IFn `deftype` that cannot be called at all, and in
;; every case the application event the author wrote is simply never
;; dispatched — after which routing proceeds and the page looks fine.
;;
;; So the boundary TRANSLATES what it accepts and REFUSES what it does
;; not, at render, on both hosts. Translation matters as much as refusal:
;; `v/handler` is the declared imperative form, and fencing without
;; unwrapping it would have left the one right answer as the one thing
;; that did not work.
;;
;; The error id and the recovery are pinned; the message is prose and is
;; matched by substring only.
{:fh/id "FH-ROUTELINK-007"
 :fh/law
 "v/route-link's :on-click is the imperative pre-navigation seam: a plain function or a v/handler is accepted and invoked exactly once before the navigation decision; an event vector, an event options map or a v/event is rejected at render, on both hosts, naming the recovery."

 :routes {:fh.route/cart "/cart"}

 :props {:to :fh.route/cart}

 ;; The two accepted spellings. Both must RUN, both must be able to veto
 ;; with .preventDefault, and — for a v/handler — the function reaching
 ;; routing must be the one the callback carries, not the callback.
 :accepted
 [{:why "a plain function — the unceremonious spelling"
   :form :bare-fn}
  {:why "a v/handler — the declared imperative form, unwrapped at the boundary"
   :form :v-handler}]

 ;; One veto arm and one non-veto arm, run for EACH accepted form. The
 ;; non-veto arm is the positive control: a contract that only proved
 ;; vetoing would pass a view that intercepted nothing at all.
 :veto-cases
 [{:why "the caller runs and does not veto — the framework still intercepts"
   :veto?       false
   :caller-runs 1
   :dispatched? true}
  {:why "the caller calls preventDefault — the framework stands down"
   :veto?       true
   :caller-runs 1
   :dispatched? false}]

 ;; A caller that does not supply :on-click at all is unaffected: the
 ;; ordinary link, unchanged by this contract.
 :absent
 {:why "no :on-click — plain navigation, untouched"
  :dispatched? true}

 ;; Everything refused, and the description the diagnostic must give of
 ;; it. `:tag` is the phrase naming WHAT was passed, so the sentence
 ;; points at the value's shape rather than only at the rule.
 :rejected
 [{:why "an event vector — Freehand's ordinary intent spelling, and the mistake this law closes"
   :form :event-vector
   :tag  "an event vector"}
  {:why "an event options map — intent plus listener options, still intent"
   :form :event-options
   :tag  "an event options map"}
  {:why "a v/event — the declared conversion seam, which is still a second intent site"
   :form :v-event
   :tag  "a v/event"}
  {:why "a v/render-fn — a pure render callback has no role at a click"
   :form :v-render-fn
   :tag  "a v/render-fn"}
  {:why "a v/raw-fn — identity-preserving, but this seam promises no identity to preserve"
   :form :v-raw-fn
   :tag  "a v/raw-fn"}
  {:why "a keyword — not a callback in any Freehand grammar"
   :form :keyword
   :tag  "a keyword"}]

 :error
 {:id          :rf.error/view-bad-event
  :where       v/route-link
  :recovery    :use-a-plain-fn-or-v-handler
  :legal-forms [:bare-fn :v-handler :nil]}

 ;; Substrings the human sentence must carry: the form that IS accepted,
 ;; the imperative work it is for, and the one intent the click already
 ;; produces. Substring, not equality — the sentence is prose.
 :message-mentions ["v/handler" ".preventDefault" ":rf.route/url-requested"]

 ;; The greppable trailing token every thrown re-frame2 error carries.
 :message-token "[:rf.error/view-bad-event]"}
